00:33:53.670 --> 00:33:57.810
- Well, I think that this
is all about perspective.

00:33:57.810 --> 00:34:01.433
You got the guy who has busted
his tire, popped his tire,

00:34:02.650 --> 00:34:05.500
who pulls in and he's
like, isn't this awesome,

00:34:05.500 --> 00:34:06.600
and this is crazy.

00:34:06.600 --> 00:34:08.860
This is crazy that I just
pulled into this tire place.

00:34:08.860 --> 00:34:10.847
And the tire guys, who
work at the tire place,

00:34:10.847 --> 00:34:11.840
"Oh, there's another guy who thinks

00:34:11.840 --> 00:34:13.780
it's a great coincidence
that he popped his tire

00:34:13.780 --> 00:34:15.920
on that curb that busts everybody's tire.

00:34:15.920 --> 00:34:16.753
You know what I'm sayin'?

00:34:16.753 --> 00:34:21.600
- I think they might have spike strips.

00:34:21.600 --> 00:34:23.180
They might be drawin'-
- It's intentional.

00:34:23.180 --> 00:34:24.540
- Yeah, they might be drawin' people in.

00:34:24.540 --> 00:34:26.075
It might be more than just a curb.

00:34:26.075 --> 00:34:27.660
You know what I'm saying?
- It wasn't just a curb,

00:34:27.660 --> 00:34:29.270
it was sabotage.
- It might be

00:34:29.270 --> 00:34:31.600
a tire Ponzi scheme,

00:34:31.600 --> 00:34:32.950
- A tire poppin' Ponzi scheme.

00:34:32.950 --> 00:34:34.420
- I bet you they didn't do that.

00:34:34.420 --> 00:34:35.660
Let's believe the best.

00:34:35.660 --> 00:34:37.993
I mean, it is a good feeling.

00:34:39.700 --> 00:34:41.330
The thing that did excite me

00:34:41.330 --> 00:34:43.850
about talking about coincidences,

00:34:43.850 --> 00:34:48.690
coinkydinks large and small
is when you experience it,

00:34:51.090 --> 00:34:52.570
there's like this elation.

00:34:52.570 --> 00:34:56.400
Oh, I'm at a tire shop,
this is so perfect.

00:34:56.400 --> 00:34:57.733
Like this is lined up.

00:34:59.050 --> 00:35:01.960
Most of the time it's like inexplicable,

00:35:01.960 --> 00:35:06.450
but for it to be so
serendipitously service-oriented

00:35:06.450 --> 00:35:07.730
is really nice.

00:35:07.730 --> 00:35:11.070
But even when it's just
kinda crazy, like, man,

00:35:11.070 --> 00:35:14.213
we were in the same wedding
as kids and now we're married.

00:35:16.150 --> 00:35:19.890
It feels cool, it feels cool, right.

00:35:19.890 --> 00:35:20.723
- Yeah.

00:35:20.723 --> 00:35:24.540
- I mean, do you remember
the main coincidence

00:35:25.530 --> 00:35:29.810
that I'd love to hear again
is the wild horses one

00:35:29.810 --> 00:35:31.710
and we can get back to the tire guy.

00:35:31.710 --> 00:35:33.730
- Okay, the wild horses story.

00:35:33.730 --> 00:35:38.140
- Because this one highlights
perfectly the thrill

00:35:38.140 --> 00:35:42.290
of experiencing a coincidence.

00:35:42.290 --> 00:35:44.820
Like you may not know the meaning,

00:35:44.820 --> 00:35:46.780
but you feel like there
should be a meaning

00:35:46.780 --> 00:35:50.510
because it's just so
crazy that it happened.

00:35:50.510 --> 00:35:53.330
And it's a shared experience
that when you tell it

00:35:53.330 --> 00:35:56.320
it's never as good as
when you experienced it.

00:35:56.320 --> 00:35:58.360
But it brings up other
people's experiences,

00:35:58.360 --> 00:36:00.000
so you, at least you can share in that.

00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:01.150
And that's what I want.

00:36:03.170 --> 00:36:06.653
- I don't remember what year
it was, let's just say 2005,

00:36:07.820 --> 00:36:09.450
because it was in the aughts.

00:36:09.450 --> 00:36:10.930
- Okay.

00:36:10.930 --> 00:36:15.150
- I'm with my brother-in-law
Chris in a boat

00:36:16.160 --> 00:36:21.160
at the North Carolina beach,
specifically in the sound

00:36:21.380 --> 00:36:23.563
between Emerald Isle and the mainland,

00:36:24.570 --> 00:36:26.600
near Beaufort, North Carolina.

00:36:26.600 --> 00:36:28.050
- Okay.

00:36:28.050 --> 00:36:31.060
- This is a new boat
that Chris has gotten,

00:36:31.060 --> 00:36:32.900
he bought from his brother, I think.

00:36:32.900 --> 00:36:35.570
So we're kind of like feeling
like this freedom of like,

00:36:35.570 --> 00:36:38.200
man, we got a boat, we can go anywhere

00:36:38.200 --> 00:36:40.520
on this intracoastal waterway.

00:36:40.520 --> 00:36:43.713
And that's not like an expression
I made up, that's a thing.

00:36:44.930 --> 00:36:49.930
So we're in the boat and you know,

00:36:49.970 --> 00:36:53.680
the coast of North Carolina
its got the Outer Banks,

00:36:53.680 --> 00:36:56.103
but it's not just the
mainland and then like,

00:36:57.930 --> 00:36:59.920
a long strip of, there's all kinds of

00:36:59.920 --> 00:37:01.430
little islands all around

00:37:01.430 --> 00:37:03.580
if you look at the map
of the coast, right?

00:37:04.430 --> 00:37:06.750
Some are small, some are big.

00:37:06.750 --> 00:37:07.970
There's just a bunch of 'em.

00:37:07.970 --> 00:37:09.690
And as we're kind of
goin' through the sound

00:37:09.690 --> 00:37:12.780
and I see these little
marsh-covered islands,

00:37:12.780 --> 00:37:14.499
some have trees on 'em.

00:37:14.499 --> 00:37:15.880
And I'm like, "You know there's an island,

00:37:15.880 --> 00:37:17.920
somewhere on the the
coast of North Carolina

00:37:17.920 --> 00:37:19.937
that has wild horses on it."

00:37:20.780 --> 00:37:21.980
And Chris is like, "No."

00:37:22.850 --> 00:37:23.900
And I'm like, "Yeah."

00:37:26.930 --> 00:37:28.290
I think it has something to do with maybe

00:37:28.290 --> 00:37:31.290
the Spanish came over
here hundreds of years ago

00:37:31.290 --> 00:37:32.900
and some horses got out

00:37:32.900 --> 00:37:35.688
and now there's like a wild population-

00:37:35.688 --> 00:37:38.500
- Undomesticated.
- A herd of wild horses

00:37:38.500 --> 00:37:40.343
that live on some island.

00:37:41.240 --> 00:37:43.760
I was like, "I don't think
it's anywhere around here.

00:37:43.760 --> 00:37:46.637
But there are wild horses somewhere."

00:37:49.200 --> 00:37:51.960
So, I mean, we moved on.

00:37:51.960 --> 00:37:53.750
We started talkin' about other things.

00:37:53.750 --> 00:37:55.740
And then we just start goin',

00:37:55.740 --> 00:37:58.530
like he's got the pedal to the metal,

00:37:58.530 --> 00:38:00.493
we're just flying through the sound.

00:38:01.960 --> 00:38:03.040
You can't hear anything.

00:38:03.040 --> 00:38:04.610
We're playin' the radio,

00:38:04.610 --> 00:38:06.040
the whole time we've
been playin' the radio,

00:38:06.040 --> 00:38:09.420
but we can't hear anything
when you're going full bore.

00:38:09.420 --> 00:38:12.260
And we're goin' past this island

00:38:14.180 --> 00:38:17.820
and all of a sudden we look to our right

00:38:17.820 --> 00:38:21.983
and out from like behind a
tree, this horse comes up,

00:38:26.936 --> 00:38:30.670
and like turns sideways so that it's like

00:38:30.670 --> 00:38:32.800
we can see the whole horse body.

00:38:32.800 --> 00:38:34.060
- Wow.
Like it's displaying

00:38:34.060 --> 00:38:36.690
itself to us.
- And what did he say?

00:38:36.690 --> 00:38:38.400
- The horse said nothing.

00:38:38.400 --> 00:38:43.200
Chris immediately stops accelerating

00:38:43.200 --> 00:38:46.823
and the boat slows down
and we're just idling.

00:38:48.224 --> 00:38:50.800
And we look at each other
like, what in the world?

00:38:50.800 --> 00:38:51.740
And he's like lookin' at me like,

00:38:51.740 --> 00:38:52.950
I can't believe this is happening.

00:38:52.950 --> 00:38:57.070
Then, because the boat is
no longer making any noise

00:38:57.070 --> 00:38:58.800
because we're idling,

00:38:58.800 --> 00:39:02.450
we hear the song that
is playing on the radio.

00:39:02.450 --> 00:39:05.040
And the song that is
currently playing on the radio

00:39:05.040 --> 00:39:07.778
is "Wild Horses" by Rolling Stones.

00:39:07.778 --> 00:39:09.460
(Link laughs)

00:39:09.460 --> 00:39:10.823
- Crazy man.

00:39:11.880 --> 00:39:12.840
That's crazy.

00:39:12.840 --> 00:39:15.169
And then do you remember what you did?

00:39:15.169 --> 00:39:17.290
You looked at each other wide-eyed right?

00:39:17.290 --> 00:39:20.960
And you were like, are we
really here for this, right?

00:39:20.960 --> 00:39:22.760
- Yeah, it was mind blowing.

00:39:22.760 --> 00:39:24.511
- It's a great feeling.

00:39:24.511 --> 00:39:27.880
- It was mind blowing.

00:39:27.880 --> 00:39:30.110
- It's a strange mixture of what?

00:39:30.110 --> 00:39:33.803
Awe, fear, there's like a
little bit of fear in it, right?

00:39:35.000 --> 00:39:38.320
You start to feel like is there meaning?

00:39:38.320 --> 00:39:39.153
- You have a choice here.

00:39:39.153 --> 00:39:40.410
- Is this a puzzle?

00:39:40.410 --> 00:39:42.160
- You have a choice in that moment.

00:39:44.060 --> 00:39:48.730
And the human tendency is to
attach meaning to it, right?

00:39:48.730 --> 00:39:49.563
- Sure.

00:39:49.563 --> 00:39:52.747
- Of course, it is questionable
what the meaning would be.

00:39:52.747 --> 00:39:55.960
(Rhett laughs) It's
like, well what is the,

00:39:55.960 --> 00:39:58.403
what is the lesson from the universe here?

00:40:00.054 --> 00:40:02.060
Am I supposed to let write
a letter to Keith Richards?

00:40:02.060 --> 00:40:04.610
I don't know what is the point?

00:40:04.610 --> 00:40:08.290
We just kinda reveled in the coincidence.

00:40:08.290 --> 00:40:12.220
And also, you know me,
and you're the same way,

00:40:12.220 --> 00:40:15.590
I'm also thinking, man, I've
got a hell of a story to tell.

00:40:15.590 --> 00:40:16.780
I can't wait 'til we get back home.

00:40:16.780 --> 00:40:18.773
- Was it just one horse.

00:40:19.700 --> 00:40:21.680
'Cause typically there's
like a whole herd.

00:40:21.680 --> 00:40:23.580
- Well, I think we ended
up seeing another one,

00:40:23.580 --> 00:40:27.530
like eventually we saw some other ones.

00:40:27.530 --> 00:40:29.100
- Sure it wasn't a deer?

00:40:29.100 --> 00:40:32.160
- It was a horse and it was
called Shackleford Island.

00:40:32.160 --> 00:40:33.140
- And that was it?

00:40:33.140 --> 00:40:35.215
- Which is known, yeah,
which is known as having-

00:40:35.215 --> 00:40:36.350
- You didn't even know that was it.

00:40:36.350 --> 00:40:37.790
And you were just shootin'
the breeze earlier.

00:40:37.790 --> 00:40:38.623
Having heard-

00:40:38.623 --> 00:40:42.980
- I had heard like a radio
story about it or somethin'.

00:40:42.980 --> 00:40:45.630
- But go with me here,
because there is somethin'

00:40:45.630 --> 00:40:50.630
to this strange mixture of
emotions associated with

00:40:51.460 --> 00:40:56.460
experiencing something,
so wild and so horse-like.

00:40:59.030 --> 00:41:01.910
- Well, I think maybe-
- There's a bit of,

00:41:01.910 --> 00:41:03.517
there's awe-
- There's a rush.

00:41:03.517 --> 00:41:07.580
- And a rush that's almost, it
feels a little bit like fear,

00:41:07.580 --> 00:41:10.640
like you're on the edge of
a building kind of a thing.

00:41:10.640 --> 00:41:12.461
There's like a physical response to it.

00:41:12.461 --> 00:41:13.970
- Well do you think,
and I don't like to be-

00:41:13.970 --> 00:41:15.360
- Like dribble pee in your pants.

00:41:15.360 --> 00:41:17.100
- I don't want to be overly reductionistic

00:41:17.100 --> 00:41:22.100
and to say that, and I know
that there are some people

00:41:22.150 --> 00:41:25.700
who try to like point
to the very specific,

00:41:25.700 --> 00:41:28.333
like evolutionary advantage that-

00:41:30.040 --> 00:41:30.960
- Of meaning-making.

00:41:30.960 --> 00:41:32.537
- That led to this.

00:41:32.537 --> 00:41:36.050
And I know that there is
a scientific explanation

00:41:36.050 --> 00:41:40.160
for the significance and also
the sort of mental response

00:41:40.160 --> 00:41:41.923
that we get to coincidence.

00:41:44.170 --> 00:41:47.300
Guessing that there's a lion in the bushes

00:41:47.300 --> 00:41:52.300
and there being a lion in
the bushes that's good.

00:41:54.020 --> 00:41:56.440
Like that helped you survive,
you know what I mean?

00:41:56.440 --> 00:41:58.140
That's probably overly simplistic.

00:41:59.560 --> 00:42:02.500
But it could have
something to do with that.

00:42:02.500 --> 00:42:07.500
That being able to recognize
and attach significance

00:42:07.500 --> 00:42:12.500
to seemingly random things
is helpful for your survival.

00:42:13.310 --> 00:42:15.470
But it could just also be
that it just is an amazing,

00:42:15.470 --> 00:42:18.040
it helps you connect socially.

00:42:18.040 --> 00:42:20.440
Because me and my
brother-in-law have this story

00:42:21.480 --> 00:42:23.150
that we'll tell.

00:42:23.150 --> 00:42:26.813
We will tell if we're in
the right place, right time.

00:42:27.810 --> 00:42:31.200
- I mean it is, when Christy
and I were on our van trip,

00:42:31.200 --> 00:42:35.110
I think that's, again, I was thinking of,

00:42:35.110 --> 00:42:39.520
I think there were wild
burros, like throughout Utah,

00:42:39.520 --> 00:42:43.510
Arizona, California, lotsa
little donkeys runnin' around

00:42:44.670 --> 00:42:46.680
in those open spaces.

00:42:46.680 --> 00:42:49.530
And it reminded me of
the story you just told.

00:42:49.530 --> 00:42:52.370
And I was tellin' Christy the story

00:42:52.370 --> 00:42:57.370
and I was playing my
Spotify playlist, and-

00:42:58.770 --> 00:43:00.973
- The song "Wild Asses" from 2 Live Crew.

00:43:02.450 --> 00:43:04.540
- The song "Wild Horses" came on

00:43:04.540 --> 00:43:08.340
as I was telling this story
and I had not played it.

00:43:08.340 --> 00:43:10.300
I didn't select the playlist

00:43:10.300 --> 00:43:12.370
knowing that it was in the playlist.

00:43:12.370 --> 00:43:16.283
It was a really long,
automatically-generated playlist.

00:43:17.860 --> 00:43:21.870
Yes, I was in '70s classic rock playlist.

00:43:21.870 --> 00:43:24.150
- But still, there's a
lot of music in that.

00:43:24.150 --> 00:43:26.500
- I mean, we weren't freakin'
out when it happened,

00:43:26.500 --> 00:43:29.460
but it it did give me a
little boost, you know?

00:43:29.460 --> 00:43:31.820
- Yeah, yeah definitely.

00:43:31.820 --> 00:43:35.040
- I think the main thing
is when you experience

00:43:36.560 --> 00:43:40.662
a coincidence don't hold back
on the celebration part of it.

00:43:40.662 --> 00:43:41.495
- Enjoy it.

00:43:41.495 --> 00:43:44.840
- To me that's the thing
like squeeze that lemon,

00:43:44.840 --> 00:43:46.553
milk it for all it's worth.

00:43:49.081 --> 00:43:50.850
Just really smoosh the teat.

00:43:50.850 --> 00:43:53.320
- Well, I think that's why-

00:43:53.320 --> 00:43:54.153
- It's fun.

00:43:55.205 --> 00:43:57.400
'Cause like when we, we'll do that a lot.

00:43:57.400 --> 00:43:59.379
We'll turn things into a coincidence.

00:43:59.379 --> 00:44:00.256
- Well, that's what I'm getting at-

00:44:00.256 --> 00:44:01.840
- This fortune cookie is tellin' us

00:44:01.840 --> 00:44:03.950
what we should do with our careers.

00:44:03.950 --> 00:44:05.767
And then our eyes'll
get big and we're like,

00:44:05.767 --> 00:44:07.558
"Gah, this is me."

00:44:07.558 --> 00:44:09.280
- But I'm talkin' about
more than just that,

00:44:09.280 --> 00:44:10.650
'cause we don't, we
were talkin' about this,

00:44:10.650 --> 00:44:12.130
we don't remember what
the fortune cookie said,

00:44:12.130 --> 00:44:14.200
but we had a significant
fortune cookie experience

00:44:14.200 --> 00:44:16.478
at some point. I don't know

00:44:16.478 --> 00:44:17.790
if I told this story.
- But not significant enough

00:44:17.790 --> 00:44:19.340
to remember.
- But also at the beach

00:44:19.340 --> 00:44:21.960
with my wife's family,
not on the same trip,

00:44:21.960 --> 00:44:26.480
but with our friend Mike,
that we've been camping with

00:44:26.480 --> 00:44:27.700
a number of times.

00:44:27.700 --> 00:44:32.700
And we were eating and we
had finished corn on the cob.

00:44:32.790 --> 00:44:35.810
And we had all these corn cobs.

00:44:35.810 --> 00:44:36.643
- Okay.

00:44:36.643 --> 00:44:40.300
- And for some reason we just got the idea

00:44:40.300 --> 00:44:42.350
that we were gonna, from the third floor,

00:44:43.295 --> 00:44:46.410
the third floor balcony
of this beach house,

00:44:46.410 --> 00:44:49.300
we were going to throw corncobs

00:44:49.300 --> 00:44:53.370
and try to get them to land
in the middle of the road

00:44:53.370 --> 00:44:56.370
between the double yellow line.

00:44:56.370 --> 00:44:57.203
- Okay, yeah.

00:45:00.300 --> 00:45:01.626
This equals-
- Littering.

00:45:01.626 --> 00:45:05.780
- A big pile of corncobs all over a road.

00:45:05.780 --> 00:45:09.560
- Unless the first one you throw-

00:45:09.560 --> 00:45:10.780
- No.

00:45:10.780 --> 00:45:14.530
- Rolls, hits the road,
and rolls and comes to rest

00:45:14.530 --> 00:45:16.190
right between the double yellow lines.

00:45:16.190 --> 00:45:17.726
- The first-
- The first one I threw.

00:45:17.726 --> 00:45:18.713
- The first one Mike threw.

00:45:18.713 --> 00:45:20.303
- The first one I threw.
- The first one Mike threw.

00:45:20.303 --> 00:45:21.630
Mike is amazing.

00:45:21.630 --> 00:45:25.450
- Mike has been with me for
about four or five weird things

00:45:25.450 --> 00:45:26.850
that have happened like that.

00:45:26.850 --> 00:45:27.920
But what I'm saying is that-

00:45:27.920 --> 00:45:29.230
- You know why?

00:45:29.230 --> 00:45:30.670
This is the great thing about Mike.

00:45:30.670 --> 00:45:34.100
- Because I knew Mike would
go nuts if it happened.

00:45:34.100 --> 00:45:36.330
- Well, but, also earlier than that.

00:45:36.330 --> 00:45:38.270
You know what? This is worthwhile.

00:45:38.270 --> 00:45:41.810
Like this stupid idea that is

00:45:42.920 --> 00:45:45.730
on the edge of impossibility is just,

00:45:45.730 --> 00:45:48.500
this is a worthwhile endeavor, you know?

00:45:48.500 --> 00:45:51.230
So I'm even celebrating that part of it.

00:45:51.230 --> 00:45:53.863
You've got someone around
who's like, you know what?

00:45:53.863 --> 00:45:56.013
This is, yeah, this is
what we're gonna do.

00:45:58.250 --> 00:45:59.620
And there's something about

00:45:59.620 --> 00:46:01.320
if you hold your mouth just right,

00:46:03.680 --> 00:46:05.550
it's like you brought
this confidence to it.

00:46:05.550 --> 00:46:08.020
I know what the experience
of that corn cob

00:46:08.020 --> 00:46:10.740
landing just right, it would be like.

00:46:10.740 --> 00:46:12.040
So, I bet you.

00:46:12.040 --> 00:46:14.010
I wasn't there, but I
bet you set it up like,

00:46:14.010 --> 00:46:16.460
we're gonna throw this.

00:46:16.460 --> 00:46:18.413
You know you're inventing something crazy.

00:46:18.413 --> 00:46:20.500
It's gonna land parallel,
right in between-

00:46:20.500 --> 00:46:22.490
- You're setting something
up that's unlikely.

00:46:22.490 --> 00:46:25.563
And so that if it does
happen, it's gonna be awesome.

00:46:27.334 --> 00:46:29.010
- That happened on the
first thing is like,

00:46:29.010 --> 00:46:33.070
I mean, I assume you
erupted in celebration

00:46:33.070 --> 00:46:34.330
for a prolonged period of time.

00:46:34.330 --> 00:46:35.870
- Of course we did and then-

00:46:35.870 --> 00:46:37.750
- And I also assume you
didn't try it any more.

00:46:37.750 --> 00:46:39.550
Or did Mike say, "I'm gonna do it too."

00:46:39.550 --> 00:46:41.370
- I can't remember, but more recently,

00:46:41.370 --> 00:46:45.560
Mike came to stay with us at
our house we live in currently,

00:46:45.560 --> 00:46:47.870
and we're like tellin'
the boys stories about,

00:46:47.870 --> 00:46:49.500
I can't remember most of 'em right now.

00:46:49.500 --> 00:46:51.380
That's the craziest one.

00:46:51.380 --> 00:46:53.083
But we've done things like that.

00:46:54.070 --> 00:46:56.550
Usually involving throwing
something at something

00:46:56.550 --> 00:46:57.970
to see what would happen.

00:46:57.970 --> 00:47:01.290
- I mean, that's a long-standing
tradition, I think.

00:47:01.290 --> 00:47:04.230
I think it's from when we were kids.

00:47:04.230 --> 00:47:06.520
- Yeah, I did a lotta thing
where I was just like,

00:47:06.520 --> 00:47:08.720
I'm gonna throw this rock at this pole

00:47:08.720 --> 00:47:10.830
and if I hit it the answer
to this question is yes.

00:47:10.830 --> 00:47:12.280
It was kind of my magic eight ball.

00:47:12.280 --> 00:47:13.560
- Yeah.
- I did a lot of that,

00:47:13.560 --> 00:47:16.930
but we were telling Locke
and Shepherd the story

00:47:16.930 --> 00:47:18.800
and then it turned into, okay,

00:47:18.800 --> 00:47:21.490
we're gonna throw the
basketball on top of the house.

00:47:21.490 --> 00:47:25.180
And it's gonna come down and bounce once

00:47:25.180 --> 00:47:30.120
and then bank into the
basket, the basketball goal.

00:47:30.120 --> 00:47:32.630
And it did not happen on the first try.

00:47:32.630 --> 00:47:34.920
But we committed and we took like an hour

00:47:34.920 --> 00:47:36.791
and we finally got it.

00:47:36.791 --> 00:47:39.200
And because our roof is terracotta,

00:47:39.200 --> 00:47:42.010
so you can't predict the
bounce of the basketball.

00:47:42.010 --> 00:47:44.423
- It's got a point though, nature.

00:47:45.880 --> 00:47:48.480
- But yeah, so I love
setting that stuff up

00:47:48.480 --> 00:47:49.860
just because it's cool.

00:47:49.860 --> 00:47:52.540
And for me it's about the connection.

00:47:52.540 --> 00:47:53.890
- Yeah.
- That's somebody else.

00:47:53.890 --> 00:47:55.277
- Yeah, that's the great thing about-

00:47:55.277 --> 00:47:57.020
- If you're just by yourself-

00:47:57.020 --> 00:47:59.210
- If it happens by yourself
it's just a little freaky.

00:47:59.210 --> 00:48:01.910
- You might connect by telling
a story about it later.

00:48:04.200 --> 00:48:06.599
- I got nothin' else to
say about the tire story.

00:48:06.599 --> 00:48:10.100
Let's go to Mythical crew members.

00:48:10.100 --> 00:48:12.930
I guess Kiko put this to crew members.

00:48:12.930 --> 00:48:14.730
Oh, maybe they follow us on Twitter.

00:48:15.850 --> 00:48:20.850
I mean, it's not a prerequisite
for working for us.

00:48:22.710 --> 00:48:24.020
Do you follow employees?

00:48:24.020 --> 00:48:25.963
Because I as a policy don't.

00:48:27.660 --> 00:48:29.060
- I have sort of-
- We haven't discussed this.

00:48:29.060 --> 00:48:32.100
- I have a general policy
of not following employees,

00:48:32.100 --> 00:48:34.950
but I realize that it
wasn't always my policy.

00:48:34.950 --> 00:48:39.850
And so there was a time in
which I did follow some people.

00:48:39.850 --> 00:48:42.050
And then it's like, if
somebody's been with us

00:48:42.050 --> 00:48:44.080
for a really long time,
sometimes I'll be like,

00:48:44.080 --> 00:48:45.550
hmm, I think I should.

00:48:45.550 --> 00:48:47.440
I don't have a good policy on this

00:48:47.440 --> 00:48:50.420
but I probably should just,
I should have a policy.

00:48:50.420 --> 00:48:52.763
You don't follow anybody as a policy?

00:48:54.600 --> 00:48:55.640
- No, I don't.

00:48:55.640 --> 00:48:58.380
- I think my policy is not great

00:48:58.380 --> 00:49:00.293
because it can open me up to,

00:49:02.880 --> 00:49:04.660
it seems like I'm showin' favoritism.

00:49:04.660 --> 00:49:07.123
- Yeah, you gotta go in
there and unfollow people.

00:49:08.120 --> 00:49:09.630
Unfollow the people who work for you.

00:49:09.630 --> 00:49:10.633
- Okay, all right.

00:49:11.775 --> 00:49:15.770
- It's a personal, professional boundary.

00:49:15.770 --> 00:49:20.470
But I mean, that being said,
you're still bein' watched.

00:49:20.470 --> 00:49:22.440
And I'm talkin' to you
Mythical crew members.

00:49:22.440 --> 00:49:23.383
- Oh, yeah, right.

00:49:25.070 --> 00:49:29.483
- Annalise producer on
Mythical Kitchen now.

00:49:30.978 --> 00:49:31.811
Okay, yeah.

00:49:32.709 --> 00:49:35.530
She tweeted a shortened version of this,

00:49:35.530 --> 00:49:36.890
but I know the complete story

00:49:36.890 --> 00:49:40.870
because I watched the Chase-hosted,

00:49:40.870 --> 00:49:45.730
Good Mythical crew
podcast that's exclusively

00:49:45.730 --> 00:49:47.270
on the Mythical Society.

00:49:47.270 --> 00:49:51.210
And Annalise was one of the producers on,

00:49:51.210 --> 00:49:54.310
like people who were fans first
and then became employees,

00:49:54.310 --> 00:49:55.360
that's the episode they did.

00:49:55.360 --> 00:49:58.370
And I really got a kick outta that.

00:49:58.370 --> 00:50:02.253
But she tells a story of,
and I'm gonna mess it up.

00:50:03.920 --> 00:50:07.120
I'm pretty sure she just moved to LA.

00:50:07.120 --> 00:50:08.430
She moved in with a roommate.

00:50:08.430 --> 00:50:12.380
She did not know this roommate,
if I remember correctly.

00:50:12.380 --> 00:50:15.380
And then she was a fan of ours

00:50:18.860 --> 00:50:23.270
and were making that Lionel Richie,

00:50:23.270 --> 00:50:25.740
we sing "All Night Long" all night long,

00:50:25.740 --> 00:50:27.160
Lionel Richie's "All Night Long."

00:50:27.160 --> 00:50:31.060
And at one point, even though
we were filmin' it constantly

00:50:31.060 --> 00:50:32.410
for like all 11 hours.

00:50:32.410 --> 00:50:34.570
At one point we go into this house party

00:50:34.570 --> 00:50:36.530
and there's all these people
that we wanted to come out.

00:50:36.530 --> 00:50:39.190
So we put a call out
to fans in the LA area

00:50:39.190 --> 00:50:43.960
who wanted to show up at
like 2:00 a.m. at this house

00:50:43.960 --> 00:50:46.230
that we were gonna walk through
singing "All Night Long."

00:50:46.230 --> 00:50:48.060
And they were gonna start
singin' along with us.

00:50:48.060 --> 00:50:50.940
And that's a big scene in the video.

00:50:50.940 --> 00:50:55.353
And Annalise is just a fan and she's like,

00:50:56.307 --> 00:51:00.700
"Oh wow, I'm just moving in today.

00:51:00.700 --> 00:51:03.630
And tonight is when I gotta go."

00:51:03.630 --> 00:51:05.730
And she's like, I know this sounds weird.

00:51:05.730 --> 00:51:06.920
You're my new roommate.

00:51:06.920 --> 00:51:09.330
And she like tells her
roommate that she's like,

00:51:09.330 --> 00:51:12.330
I'm fans of these guys,
they're doing this music video.

00:51:12.330 --> 00:51:15.900
So I'm gonna be going to this
house party, music video thing

00:51:15.900 --> 00:51:18.377
at like 3:00 a.m.

00:51:18.377 --> 00:51:21.295
And her roommate's like,
"I'll go with you."

00:51:21.295 --> 00:51:22.460
And this was like when they first met.

00:51:22.460 --> 00:51:23.507
So they both go.

00:51:23.507 --> 00:51:25.497
And yeah, she tweeted, "I
was at Rhett and Link's

00:51:25.497 --> 00:51:30.497
"All Night Long" 4:00 a.m.
house party playing a kazoo

00:51:30.600 --> 00:51:32.817
and now I work for them."

00:51:32.817 --> 00:51:34.010
We did not hire her that night.

00:51:34.010 --> 00:51:35.350
We didn't meet her that night,

00:51:35.350 --> 00:51:37.290
but you can see her in the music video.

00:51:37.290 --> 00:51:39.980
- Somebody had to coordinate
what the crowd would do

00:51:39.980 --> 00:51:42.900
when we walked in because we
could not walk, we couldn't-

00:51:42.900 --> 00:51:45.100
- Yeah, there were people,
we had someone there

00:51:45.100 --> 00:51:47.773
who was like organizing
the crowd and getting them-

00:51:47.773 --> 00:51:50.334
- So when we walked in-
- Getting them to rehearse.

00:51:50.334 --> 00:51:51.167
Getting them to rehearse and everything.

00:51:51.167 --> 00:51:54.060
- Because we left the house,
went into the front yard

00:51:54.060 --> 00:51:55.710
and then went out on the street.

00:51:55.710 --> 00:51:57.170
- And they followed us down the street.

00:51:57.170 --> 00:51:59.300
- In the middle of the
night in this neighbor.

00:51:59.300 --> 00:52:03.580
- Yeah, and then we got
in a car and kept goin'.

00:52:03.580 --> 00:52:05.133
- I'm sure we made some people mad.

00:52:05.133 --> 00:52:07.020
Well, Annalise is in the video, right?

00:52:07.020 --> 00:52:08.960
- Yeah.
- If you watch that scene

00:52:08.960 --> 00:52:09.793
you can see her in the video.

00:52:09.793 --> 00:52:12.120
- And then years later, years later.

00:52:12.120 --> 00:52:13.420
- 'Cause that was a long time ago.

00:52:13.420 --> 00:52:15.614
- Yeah, she works for us.

00:52:15.614 --> 00:52:16.741
- I think that might be magic.

00:52:16.741 --> 00:52:21.290
- And she did not tell the
story in her job interview,

00:52:21.290 --> 00:52:22.270
which wasn't with us.

00:52:22.270 --> 00:52:24.940
So she didn't get any
brownie points for that.

00:52:24.940 --> 00:52:27.350
- You know what, it's probably wise to not

00:52:27.350 --> 00:52:29.610
tell that kinda story
in your job interview.

00:52:29.610 --> 00:52:31.280
- Yeah.
- Like I got a weird call

00:52:31.280 --> 00:52:32.640
from Rhett and Link years ago

00:52:32.640 --> 00:52:35.550
to show up at a house
at 4:00 a.m. and I did.

00:52:35.550 --> 00:52:37.743
- Unless you showed up on a wild horse.

00:52:38.950 --> 00:52:39.783
Read Jenna's.

00:52:40.704 --> 00:52:41.750
- Yeah, I gotta bring this closer to me

00:52:41.750 --> 00:52:44.653
because the text is small.

00:52:45.970 --> 00:52:50.207
Jenna, who works for us.

00:52:50.207 --> 00:52:54.463
"LOL, I have good coincidence
story the guys might like.

00:52:55.590 --> 00:52:58.610
A few years ago, after takin'
a break from assistant work

00:52:58.610 --> 00:53:00.420
for about a year, I thought to myself

00:53:00.420 --> 00:53:03.150
that I was ready to maybe assist again

00:53:03.150 --> 00:53:05.750
if I liked who I worked for.

00:53:05.750 --> 00:53:07.070
I didn't tell anyone."

00:53:07.070 --> 00:53:07.947
- Didn't tell anyone.

00:53:07.947 --> 00:53:10.820
- "The next day, my best
friend Laura, texted me

00:53:10.820 --> 00:53:15.660
about these two men
(both chuckle) whose hair

00:53:15.660 --> 00:53:18.230
and makeup she was doing for their show,

00:53:18.230 --> 00:53:20.560
talking about needing an assistant.

00:53:20.560 --> 00:53:22.430
She threw my name out there.

00:53:22.430 --> 00:53:25.167
Those men were Rhett and Link."

00:53:25.167 --> 00:53:26.000
- What!

00:53:26.000 --> 00:53:29.533
- First of all, I just appreciate
she's sayin' those men.

00:53:30.720 --> 00:53:35.053
We get called boys a
lot and sometimes guys.

00:53:36.100 --> 00:53:37.493
Those men Rhett and Link.

00:53:38.660 --> 00:53:41.720
- Well, that's part of
her job requirement.

00:53:41.720 --> 00:53:43.760
- Yeah, we told her
that she has to call us-

00:53:43.760 --> 00:53:45.200
- Remember one of the
first things you told her,

00:53:45.200 --> 00:53:46.816
is that always refer to us as men.

00:53:46.816 --> 00:53:47.649
- The men.

00:53:47.649 --> 00:53:50.400
- 'Cause somebody needs
to. (Link chuckles)

00:53:50.400 --> 00:53:51.710
- Well, this is a coincidence

00:53:51.710 --> 00:53:53.520
that I'm very glad it worked out, in fact-

00:53:53.520 --> 00:53:54.837
- I mean, she didn't tell anybody.

00:53:54.837 --> 00:53:56.270
She didn't even tell Laura-

00:53:56.270 --> 00:53:59.343
- In her job interview,
well, first of all,

00:54:00.870 --> 00:54:03.210
she was recommended by
Laura, who we trusted

00:54:03.210 --> 00:54:04.540
and spent a lot of time with.

00:54:04.540 --> 00:54:08.570
And especially when it
comes to having someone

00:54:08.570 --> 00:54:09.700
who's gonna be-
- Touch your face.

00:54:09.700 --> 00:54:11.390
- Well, I was gonna move beyond that,

00:54:11.390 --> 00:54:13.523
say somebody who's
gonna be your assistant.

00:54:14.560 --> 00:54:17.610
There's got to be the ability to kind of

00:54:17.610 --> 00:54:19.560
click with them personally.

00:54:19.560 --> 00:54:21.150
- Yeah.

00:54:21.150 --> 00:54:23.530
- Not only they have to
be good at their job,

00:54:23.530 --> 00:54:25.400
but you need to be able
to get along with them.

00:54:25.400 --> 00:54:29.800
And Laura just kind of saw, she was like,

00:54:29.800 --> 00:54:31.740
you know who would be perfect for this?

00:54:31.740 --> 00:54:36.740
And so that did a lot to kinda
prepare us for liking Jenna.

00:54:38.290 --> 00:54:41.740
And then her job interview was essentially

00:54:41.740 --> 00:54:44.990
coming to the Mythical
Christmas party that year.

00:54:44.990 --> 00:54:46.970
- She was Laura's date-
- As Laura's plus one.

00:54:46.970 --> 00:54:48.010
- Yeah.

00:54:48.010 --> 00:54:53.010
And of course, I felt a
little bit awkward about

00:54:53.720 --> 00:54:56.210
turning meeting her at this
party into her interview.

00:54:56.210 --> 00:54:57.200
But of course, you didn't.

00:54:57.200 --> 00:54:58.840
- No, I didn't. (Rhett laughs)

00:54:58.840 --> 00:55:02.160
I was like, what else am I gonna do here?

00:55:02.160 --> 00:55:03.880
- So we kind of conducted the interview.

00:55:03.880 --> 00:55:04.713
- Let's get this over with.

00:55:04.713 --> 00:55:08.250
- In a loud bar, where there
was a guy in the corner

00:55:08.250 --> 00:55:10.370
doing bonsai, because we
thought that would be funny.

00:55:10.370 --> 00:55:12.600
- It was kinda nice because I mean,

00:55:12.600 --> 00:55:14.750
Christy was there for it
and then Christy ended up

00:55:14.750 --> 00:55:17.300
talkin' to her and Laura more.

00:55:17.300 --> 00:55:18.440
And so it was like-

00:55:18.440 --> 00:55:21.550
- Because Jessie and Christy
needed to also like Jenna.

00:55:21.550 --> 00:55:23.623
- Yeah, that's true.

00:55:23.623 --> 00:55:25.490
- 'Cause she was gonna
be working with them too.

00:55:25.490 --> 00:55:26.424
And it all worked out

00:55:26.424 --> 00:55:28.610
- But I didn't know that
Jenna wasn't looking

00:55:28.610 --> 00:55:33.610
for an assistant job until like,
in her mind the day before.

00:55:34.324 --> 00:55:35.357
- Right.

00:55:35.357 --> 00:55:37.230
- And you know what, it's worked out.

00:55:37.230 --> 00:55:40.510
Kiko producer of this podcast,

00:55:40.510 --> 00:55:44.853
who's sitting behind in
the glass booth over there.

00:55:46.937 --> 00:55:48.830
"Four years ago, it was the Friday

00:55:48.830 --> 00:55:51.370
before I was gonna start
my new job at Mythical,

00:55:51.370 --> 00:55:53.870
I had the day off, so I
hit up my college friend

00:55:53.870 --> 00:55:56.690
of 10 years to see if
she wanted to hang out.

00:55:56.690 --> 00:56:00.060
She told me that she was
waiting for a meeting

00:56:00.060 --> 00:56:03.430
with this company that she
had been freelancing for

00:56:03.430 --> 00:56:05.050
for the past few weeks.

00:56:05.050 --> 00:56:07.700
And she was gonna find
out if she was going to be

00:56:07.700 --> 00:56:09.450
brought on full time.

00:56:09.450 --> 00:56:10.880
I got really excited and told her

00:56:10.880 --> 00:56:13.830
I was gonna start a new job on Monday too.

00:56:13.830 --> 00:56:16.610
I asked what company
to which she responded,

00:56:16.610 --> 00:56:19.560
Mythical Entertainment.
- Mythical Entertainment.

00:56:19.560 --> 00:56:22.210
Although Meggie and I have
had many jobs together,

00:56:22.210 --> 00:56:26.320
this was the first where we
both got hired separately

00:56:26.320 --> 00:56:27.663
without the other knowing.

00:56:29.850 --> 00:56:32.257
Somehow we can't seem to
get rid of each other."

00:56:33.484 --> 00:56:35.450
Yeah, Kiko will throw Meggie into a story.

00:56:35.450 --> 00:56:37.750
Meggie was in Kiko's ghost story.

00:56:37.750 --> 00:56:39.030
- A magic story too.

00:56:39.030 --> 00:56:40.630
- Oh yeah, yeah.

00:56:40.630 --> 00:56:43.400
Lots of powers at work in Kiko's-

00:56:43.400 --> 00:56:47.640
- Well I would say, the
based on the precedent

00:56:47.640 --> 00:56:50.400
of the ghost story that I can't just

00:56:50.400 --> 00:56:52.163
dismiss this as coincidence.

00:56:53.370 --> 00:56:55.730
There could be something
cosmic going on here.

00:56:55.730 --> 00:56:58.210
- I think that we just
put out job postings

00:56:58.210 --> 00:57:01.230
and like they're friends
and they apply for

00:57:01.230 --> 00:57:03.030
- the same stuff.
- Ah, don't minimize it.

00:57:03.030 --> 00:57:03.863
Don't minimize it.

00:57:03.863 --> 00:57:05.748
- Come on, Kiko, this is not.

00:57:05.748 --> 00:57:07.930
I mean, like you said, you
worked together a lot before.

00:57:07.930 --> 00:57:10.073
This is the first time you didn't know it.

00:57:10.073 --> 00:57:11.050
This is not-
- Yeah but think about it

00:57:11.050 --> 00:57:11.883
the name of the company.

00:57:11.883 --> 00:57:13.030
- This is just-
- The name of the company

00:57:13.030 --> 00:57:15.340
is Mythical Entertainment.

00:57:15.340 --> 00:57:16.173
- Okay, okay.

00:57:16.173 --> 00:57:18.120
- That alone it gets you
to thinkin' a little bit.

00:57:18.120 --> 00:57:18.953
- You're right.

00:57:18.953 --> 00:57:21.828
Two men.
- There's two men who run it.

00:57:21.828 --> 00:57:25.128
(Link chuckles)

00:57:25.128 --> 00:57:26.145
- Two men.

00:57:26.145 --> 00:57:27.890
(Rhett laughs)

00:57:27.890 --> 00:57:28.920
- WillowHub.

00:57:28.920 --> 00:57:30.770
I'm sorry, Kiko,

00:57:30.770 --> 00:57:31.603
You know what?

00:57:31.603 --> 00:57:34.030
That was pretty awesome.

00:57:34.030 --> 00:57:35.163
- I think is awesome.

00:57:36.590 --> 00:57:37.777
- WillowHub tweeted at us.

00:57:37.777 --> 00:57:39.930
"Once I got a piece of
paper and wrote down

00:57:39.930 --> 00:57:42.457
what I would say if I was a teacher."

00:57:43.316 --> 00:57:44.481
I don't know if that's-
- But the specifics

00:57:44.481 --> 00:57:45.370
have not been provided.

00:57:45.370 --> 00:57:48.200
- I don't know if that's
a result of an assignment

00:57:48.200 --> 00:57:49.927
or like a prompt.

00:57:49.927 --> 00:57:54.927
"That class period, the
teacher copied my exact words.

00:57:56.290 --> 00:57:59.850
It was almost like she
was reading my paper."

00:57:59.850 --> 00:58:02.977
But of course she couldn't
because I still had it, I guess.

00:58:02.977 --> 00:58:06.420
"I freaked out, and the
recess, I was super nervous.

00:58:06.420 --> 00:58:08.620
